# Deploy an app to Javo from this computer

Javo runs web apps, notebooks and published container images for a
person, at {{.URL}}. You deploy the app in the person's folder for them
with only curl and tar, through the Javo API, acting as that person.

Rules for every step:

- Never print the token, write it to a file, put it in a command you show
  the person, or commit it. Keep it only in the shell variable JAVO_TOKEN.
  If your shell does not keep variables from one command to the next, run
  the login and the calls that need the token in one shell session.
- Never ask the person for a password, API key or other secret value in the
  chat, a file or a command.
- Send `X-Javo-Request: 1` with every call that is not a GET.
- Each call uses `curl -fsS`, which stops on an error answer and prints only
  its status. To read the error, run the same call with
  `-sS --fail-with-body` in place of `-fsS`. Errors are JSON problems:
  `title` and `detail` say what went wrong, and `errors` names the field.
- The whole API, with every field, is the OpenAPI document at
  {{.URL}}/api/openapi.json.

## 1. Log in

Start a login. It needs no token:

    curl -fsS -X POST -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" "{{.URL}}/api/auth/device"

The answer has `deviceCode`, `userCode`, `verificationURL`,
`verificationURLComplete`, `expiresIn` (seconds, 600) and `interval`
(seconds between polls, 5). Keep `deviceCode` to yourself.

Show the person the link `verificationURLComplete` and the code `userCode`,
and ask them to open the link, check that the page shows the same code, and
approve. They have `expiresIn` seconds.

Then poll for the token every `interval` seconds. This loop does it, keeps
the token in JAVO_TOKEN without printing it, and stops when the person
approves or denies, or the request expires. Put the answer's values in the
first two lines:

    DEVICE_CODE='<deviceCode>'
    INTERVAL=<interval>
    while :; do
      sleep "$INTERVAL"
      ANSWER=$(curl -sS -X POST -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
        -d "{\"deviceCode\":\"$DEVICE_CODE\"}" "{{.URL}}/api/auth/device/token")
      case "$ANSWER" in
        *'"token"'*) JAVO_TOKEN=$(printf '%s' "$ANSWER" | sed -n 's/.*"token":"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p'); echo "logged in"; break ;;
        *authorization_pending*) ;;
        *slow_down*) INTERVAL=$((INTERVAL + 5)) ;;
        *) printf '%s\n' "$ANSWER"; break ;;
      esac
    done

While the person has not decided, the poll answers 400 with `error`
`authorization_pending`: keep polling. `slow_down` means you polled too
soon: wait 5 seconds longer each time. `access_denied` means the person
said no, and `expired_token` means the request expired or the token was
already given: stop, and start a new login only if the person asks.

The token acts as the person, with read and write access, for 8 hours or
less. Send it as `Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN`.

## 2. Look around

    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/me"
    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/templates"
    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/sizes"
    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces"

- `/api/me` is the person you act as.
- `/api/templates`: `.items[]` with `name`, `description`, `kind`,
  `fileExtensions`, `userCommand`, `userImage`, `port` and `basePath`.
- `/api/sizes`: `.items[]` with `name`, `cpu`, `memory` (bytes), `default`
  and `fits`.
- `/api/workspaces` is the person's apps. An app is called a workspace in
  the API.

## 3. Pick a template from the files

Read the files in the folder. Any template in `/api/templates` can run
them: match the files to a template's `fileExtensions` and `description`.
A single HTML file, such as an HTML page or artifact, runs on the template
whose `fileExtensions` include `.html`. Anything else that serves HTTP runs
on `custom` with a `command`.

Packages: the app gets its template's packages, which are pinned and hold
its framework and common data packages; prefer what is there. Or it runs in
a pixi environment from `GET /api/environments` (`"environment":{"id":"<id>"}`
when you create it). Or, on `custom`, its `command` brings what it needs.

The app must listen on the port in `$PORT` on all addresses, and serve
under its base path (`$BASE_URL`). A command may use `{{"{{.Port}}"}}` and
`{{"{{.BaseURL}}"}}` for them. With `basePath: Prefix` requests arrive with
that prefix: pass it to the framework. With `Strip` the prefix is removed,
the app serves at `/`, and the prefix is in the `X-Forwarded-Prefix`
header.

To run a published container image instead of the folder, use the
`container` template with `image` (an exact version, never `latest`) and
`port`. It runs as the person's user, never root, so it cannot bind a port
below 1024. If no template has `userImage: true`, images are turned off:
tell the person.

## 4. Upload the folder

Pick a short name for the app: lower case letters, digits and dashes. Run
this in the app's folder. It sends the folder, without `.git`,
`node_modules`, `.venv` and other build output, to `deploys/<short-name>`
in the person's home folder on Javo. Add `--exclude` for any other build
output or large data the app does not need to run (for example `dist`,
`build`, `__pycache__`, `.next`):

    tar czf - --exclude .git --exclude node_modules --exclude .venv . | curl -fsS -X PUT -T - -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Content-Type: application/gzip" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/files/archive?path=deploys/<short-name>"

The answer is `path`, `files` and `bytes`.

- 201: uploaded.
- 409: `deploys/<short-name>` already exists. Ask the person whether to
  replace it. Only if they say yes, upload again with `&replace=true` at
  the end of the address. Otherwise pick another short name.
- 413: too large (100 MiB unpacked by default) or too many files. Leave
  out what the app does not need to run.
- 422: the archive holds something refused, named in the problem: only
  plain files and folders are allowed, with no links, no absolute paths
  and no `..`. Leave it out, or copy the file in place of the link.

## 5. Create the app

    curl -fsS -X POST -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"displayName":"<Title>","template":"<template>","file":"deploys/<short-name>/<file>"}' "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces"

`file` is the file the template opens, or the folder. For `custom`:

    curl -fsS -X POST -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"displayName":"<Title>","template":"custom","file":"deploys/<short-name>","command":"python server.py --port {{"{{.Port}}"}}"}' "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces"

The command runs in the folder of `file`, without a shell. `name` in the
answer is the app's short name: NAME below. Optional fields: `size` (a name
from `/api/sizes`, leave it out for the default), `idleStopMinutes`, `env`
(plain values only: `[{"name":"LOG_LEVEL","value":"info"}]`) and
`environment`.

## 6. Wait, read the logs, fix and retry

    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces/NAME"
    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces/NAME/logs?tail=200"
    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces/NAME/diagnosis"
    curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces/NAME/events"

Get the app every few seconds until `phase` is `Running`. The phases are
`Pending`, `Starting`, `Running`, `Stopped` and `Failed`. A first start can
take a few minutes (installing an environment, pulling an image).

When `phase` is `Failed` or a step in `steps` stays `False`, read that
step's `title`, `what` and `todo`, its log at
`/api/workspaces/NAME/steps/STEP/log` (STEP is the step's `type`), the
app's logs (add `&previous=true` after a crash) and `diagnosis`, the cause
Javo found and what may fix it.

- `Started` failed or keeps restarting: read the logs. A permission error
  means it wants root or a folder it cannot write. Only the home folder,
  `/tmp` and `/scratch` are writable.
- `Ready` stays `False`: the app does not listen on the port. Check the
  port and that it listens on all addresses.
- `Started` with reason `OutOfMemory`: pick a bigger size with
  `PUT /api/workspaces/NAME/size` and `{"size":"<size>"}`.

Fix the files, upload again (step 7), restart, and check again:

    curl -fsS -X POST -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces/NAME/restart"

When it is `Running`, give the person the app's `url`.

## 7. Redeploy

Upload the folder again to the same `deploys/<short-name>` with
`&replace=true` at the end of the address, then restart the app:

    tar czf - --exclude .git --exclude node_modules --exclude .venv . | curl -fsS -X PUT -T - -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Content-Type: application/gzip" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/files/archive?path=deploys/<short-name>&replace=true"
    curl -fsS -X POST -H "X-Javo-Request: 1" -H "Authorization: Bearer $JAVO_TOKEN" "{{.URL}}/api/workspaces/NAME/restart"

Replace only a folder you uploaded for this app in this session, or one the
person told you to replace. Then wait for `Running` as in step 6.

## 8. Secrets

When the app needs a secret (an API key, a database password), never ask for
its value and never put one in `env`, a command or a file. Create the app
without it. Then ask the person to store it in Settings > Secrets in the
browser and add it to the app's environment there. `GET /api/secret-names`
lists the names of the secrets they have, never the values.

## Stop when idle, and share

`PUT /api/workspaces/NAME/idle-stop` with `{"idleStopMinutes":60}` stops an
unused app after that long, within what `GET /api/idle-stop` allows.

Sharing is the person's to do in the app's sharing settings in the browser.
`GET /api/workspaces/NAME/sharing` shows the current setting.

To delete an app: `DELETE /api/workspaces/NAME`. The home folder is kept.
